i have a copy of the full report  234 pages of devastating analysis of  what was going on at this trust for more  than two decades and within this report  there are testimonies from staff members  who talk about how they felt that they  should always avoid doing caesareans how  the trust was proud of its low rate of  caesarean sections and indeed donna  ockendon  in some other comments that she's given  on publication of this report has said  that in some cases uh where a caesarean  wasn't given um if if the trust had  indeed intervened had indeed  given a mother a cesarean section babies  could have lived so this is one of the  causes of some of these deaths at the  trust but there were  multiple failings as donna ockendon has  been outlining this was a trust that  failed over many years to learn from  mistakes they failed to properly risk  assess mothers they failed to listen to  the concerns of mothers  and then when babies died they would  blame the mothers for their own baby's  deaths donna ockendon said that this  failure in care carried on for a  prolonged period of time she talked  about staff having told her that they  felt that they could not  speak up within the trust that they  tried to raise concerns but that they  were simply unable to staff who would  cry on shift because of what was going  on  in this trust but we know from her  report that initially when she was asked  to investigate back in 2017 there were  23 cases highlighted as being of concern  now over the years since she's been  investigating so many families contacted  her that in fact the testimonies of 1486  families went into this report and she's  concluded from the cases that she looked  at  210 deaths could have been avoided that  includes 201 deaths of babies  131 stillbirths could have been avoided  70 neonatal deaths could have been  avoided and also the deaths of nine  mothers could have been avoided if only  better care had been provided at this  trust the report also details how 94 uh  babies are now living with long-term  brain damage or cerebral palsy as a  result  of the failures in care at this trust  and  don ochonden said that her report  primarily looks at the period between  the year 2000 and 2019 but she said  she's deeply concerned that her team are  still being contacted by families who  are receiving poor care now  babies still dying  up until very recently at this trust  this is not  something that is in the past she said  she's concerned about what is still  going on and she has set out a whole  series of things that both this trust  and maternity units right across the  country need to do  to improve safety it includes better  monitoring of babies it includes more  wardrobes by doctors it includes  listening when things go wrong learning  when things go wrong now we have had  some reaction to this report from the  health secretary sajid javid and i'll  read you his response now he says donna  ockendon's report  paints a tragic and harrowing picture of  repeated failures and care over two  decades and i am deeply sorry to all the  families who've suffered so greatly  since the initial report was published  in 2020 we've taken steps to invest in  maternity services and grow the  workforce and we'll make the changes  that are needed so that no families have  to go through this pain again i would  like to thank donna ocondon and her  whole team for their work throughout  this long and distressing inquiry  as well as all the families who came  forward to tell their stories  donna ockendon was at pains to thank  those families it is only due to them  really having to shout to be heard that  we know about what was going on at this  trust because over many years there were  attempts made within the trust to  silence those families but they refused  to believe that their babies had to die  and now we know the true extent of what  was going on at this trust
 

